 there in you  can really see just what a meteoric  rise and fall this was tell us more yes  that's right elizabeth holmes uh who  very very uh self-consciously modeled  herself on steve jobs the co-founder of  apple even down to wearing a black  turtleneck sweater she was a college  dropout left college at the age of 19  and founded theranose thereafter so  a a real success story in terms of  silicon valley she attracted a  glittering array of investors among them  betsy devos a former u.s education  secretary her family invested tens of  millions of dollars in the business  another investor was rupert murdoch the  billionaire media tycoon and another was  one of the heirs of stavros niarcos the  greek shipping tycoon all of those  people put money into the business and  indeed betsy devos was one of the  investors who was named in the  indictment and uh there was a veneer of  respectability provided for this company  uh theranose by the number of people  that were on its board they had no fear  than two former u.s secretaries of state  dr henry kissinger and george schultz  two very very distinguished u.s  diplomats both of them were on the board  of theranos and it was really an  investigation by the wall street journal  that brought the business to its knees  the journal  had ran a number of investigative  articles by a reporter called john  carrera who went on to write a very  successful award-winning book bad blood  about the crisis and it was that that  really got people questioning as to  whether all of the produce that theranos  was making were really cracked up to it  an interesting aspect to this case by  the way is the fact that there were also  uh indictments  on whether  patients were deceived not just  investors but patients and interestingly  enough the jurors found elizabeth holmes  not guilty on that it's been suggested  that's because there wasn't sufficient  evidence available for prosecutors to  make a conviction a deliberate jury get  a jury to